We can use the tangent addition formula $\tan(A+B) = \frac{\tan A + \tan B}{1 - \tan A \tan B}$ to simplify the expression.
In this case, we have $A=20^\circ$ and $B=25^\circ$, so
\begin{align*}
\left(1 + \tan 20^\circ\right) \left(1 + \tan 25^\circ\right) &= \frac{1 + \tan 20^\circ + \tan 25^\circ + \tan 20^\circ \tan 25^\circ}{1 - \tan 20^\circ \tan 25^\circ} \\
&= \frac{\tan 20^\circ + \tan 25^\circ + \tan 20^\circ \tan 25^\circ}{1 - \tan 20^\circ \tan 25^\circ}.
\end{align*}
We can use the tangent addition formula again to simplify the numerator:
\begin{align*}
\tan 20^\circ + \tan 25^\circ &= \frac{\tan 20^\circ + \tan 25^\circ}{1 - \tan 20^\circ \tan 25^\circ} \\
&= \tan(20^\circ + 25^\circ) \\
&= \tan 45^\circ \\
&= 1.
\end{align*}
Therefore, the original expression simplifies to
\begin{align*}
\left(1 + \tan 20^\circ\right) \left(1 + \tan 25^\circ\right) &= \frac{1 + \tan 20^\circ + \tan 25^\circ + \tan 20^\circ \tan 25^\circ}{1 - \tan 20^\circ \tan 25^\circ} \\
&= \frac{1 + 1 \cdot 1}{1 - 1} \\
&= \boxed{2}.
\end{align*}
The answer is: 2

